4. Row-tagging boundary conditions (exact-equal floor; baseline row with its
   own saved floor >= request; qualifying row never tagged):
   - Read guardrailOptimizerPolicies (analysis/guardrail_optimizer.go:165):
     every one of the 64 grid candidates is constructed with
     Guardrails.MinMonthlySpendingReal == the REQUESTED floor exactly and
     Enabled=true, so the handler's disjunction (Guardrails==nil ||
     !Enabled || MinMonthlySpendingReal < floor) is structurally FALSE for
     every grid candidate — confirmed with an httptest probe on the "below"
     fixture (guardrailPlanFloorFixture): all 64 grid rows render "Meets
     target" (100.00%) and ONLY the two Baseline rows ("current" with
     Guardrails=nil per the fixture, and "no-guardrails" with Enabled=false)
     get "Below target — planned spending is below your minimum from year
     1". Re-verified this isn't a coincidence: floorAdjustedLiving
     (engine/spending_floor.go:18) computes
     adjusted = max(planned*multiplier, MinMonthlySpendingReal*cpi), so a
     candidate whose floor equals the request structurally cannot dip below
     it from plan design alone — any non-qualification for such a candidate
     is genuine funding/market risk, exactly matching the notice's own text
     ("Policies without an absolute floor at or above your minimum..."). The
     strict "< floor" (not "<=") in the handler condition is therefore
     correct by construction, not merely untested. Deleted the probe test
     afterward.
   - Qualifying rows: the condition requires !c.Qualifies first, so a
     qualifying row is never tagged regardless of its Guardrails — visible
     directly in the code (handlers_guardrail_optimizer.go:172).

